import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,ag as o}from"./chunks/framework.DC2M1AAK.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Marg and other frameworks","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"get-started/compare.md","filePath":"get-started/compare.md"}'),s={name:"get-started/compare.md"};function n(d,e,i,h,l,c){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="marg-and-other-frameworks" tabindex="-1">Marg and other frameworks <a class="header-anchor" href="#marg-and-other-frameworks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Marg and other frameworks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Marg is not a replacement for a general agent framework. It is the layer you would otherwise write on top of one when the agent has to finish a job for real users: collect what the backend needs, validate it, show it, get an explicit yes, write exactly once.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Orchestration libraries (graphs, handoffs)</th><th>Low-code builders</th><th>Marg</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who reads &quot;yes&quot;</td><td>the model</td><td>the model</td><td>the runtime, by the whole message</td></tr><tr><td>Forms, pickers, confirmation</td><td>your UI code, if any</td><td>the builder&#39;s</td><td>drawn and read by the runtime, carried into tool calls</td></tr><tr><td>Many domains, one entry point</td><td>you wire it</td><td>one bot per flow</td><td>drop-in domains, typed routing, sticky</td></tr><tr><td>Decisions</td><td>prompt and parse</td><td>prompt and parse</td><td>rules → decision model → logprobs, calibrated</td></tr><tr><td>Regression gate</td><td>your harness</td><td>manual</td><td>scenario runner, mock model, CI with no key</td></tr><tr><td>Identity, limits, caps, cost, telemetry</td><td>bring your own</td><td>platform&#39;s</td><td>included, model-agnostic</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="where-the-line-is" tabindex="-1">Where the line is <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-the-line-is" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the line is&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Tool confirmation.</strong> Most frameworks offer a confirmation hook: a tool pauses, the model asks the user, a dialog answers. The model still reads the reply. On a blind corpus of 521 typed replies, a reader that looked at the leading word mis-read 235 as a bare yes or no; reading the whole message mis-read none. Marg&#39;s gate is the runtime&#39;s, three times over: the skill rule, the tool schema, the runtime guard. <a href="/marg/build/pattern#5-the-write-gate-three-times">The pattern</a>.</p><p><strong>Human input.</strong> A graph node that pauses for input still leaves the question&#39;s wording, the choice of control and the reading of the answer to the model or to your code. In Marg a step draws its ask (a form card, chips, a picker), reads the reply by its whole content, carries the value into every later tool call, and tells the model one fact. Three helpers cover most steps. <a href="/marg/build/new-step">A new step</a>.</p><p><strong>Evaluation.</strong> Trajectory and rubric evaluation judge a run after the fact, often with another model. Marg&#39;s scenarios assert what must <em>always</em> hold, deterministically, against a writes-off gateway, on a scripted mock model, so the gate runs in CI with no key. Both have their place; Marg ships the one that can be a merge gate. <a href="/marg/build/evals">Evals</a>.</p><p><strong>Breadth.</strong> Graph workflows, agent-to-agent protocols, live audio, visual builders and a hundred integrations are not Marg&#39;s job. Marg reaches those ecosystems through MCP (your tools, any language) and the <a href="/marg/components/client-protocol">client protocol</a> (any client). Use the general framework for the general part; put Marg where the write happens.</p><p><a href="/marg/community/reviews/adk-dev-2026-10">The review of adk.dev</a> goes through one of them in detail.</p>',9)])])}const g=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
